INT. COACHING INN - COMMON ROOM - NIGHT (1815)

Low beams. A roaring fire. Pewter mugs, wet cloaks steaming on pegs.

MRS. PETTIGREW, 50s, the innkeeper, wipes the counter with her apron. MR. CRABTREE, 60s, a stout merchant, snores in the corner settle, his hat over his face. GOODY VANE, 70s, shawled, sips a thin soup. A SOLDIER in a mud-caked red coat sits by the fire with his boots off, staring into the flames, one arm bandaged to the elbow.

PETTIGREW
More ale, sir?

SOLDIER
Please.

She pours. He drinks in one gulp.

VANE
(to the room)
They say Bonaparte's loose again.

CRABTREE
(waking, muttering)
They always say that.

PETTIGREW
Hush, both of you. The man is tired.

SOLDIER
I am. But I'll answer.

He stands. The room goes quiet. The fire pops.

SOLDIER (CONT'D)
My name is Elijah Rowe. Sergeant, Thirty-third Foot. I walked from Brussels in four days.

VANE
(softly)
Brussels?

ELIJAH
Yes, mother. And I will tell you one thing true. The Emperor is not loose. He is beaten.

CRABTREE
(sitting up, awed)
Beaten?

ELIJAH
At a place called Waterloo. Yesterday.

The room breathes out all at once. Pettigrew crosses herself. Vane lets her spoon fall. Crabtree rises, takes off his hat and holds it to his chest.

ELIJAH (CONT'D)
(to himself)
I wish it felt like a victory.
